From: Airtype downstream
Subject: [PATCH] mtmd : accumulate projector matmuls in F32

The CUDA backend multiplies F16 weights through cuBLAS with CUBLAS_COMPUTE_16F and an F16
output by default (ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas picks the F16 compute type for F16 and quantized
weights on hardware with fast FP16). Vision towers produce activations beyond the F16 range, so
on large images the projector's embeddings come out partly NaN and the language model answers
with one repeated token, whatever the image shows. The CPU backend always accumulates in F32,
which is why the same projector is correct on the CPU.

LightOnOCR-2-1B (Pixtral-style tower, 8580 patches for a 200 dpi page) shows it on every page
tried: the first NaN appears in the last layer's feed-forward down projection (ffn_out-23),
whose inputs are finite, and 118 of the 2145 image tokens end up NaN. Running every graph node
through the eval callback confirms it, as does GGML_CUDA_CUBLAS_COMPUTE_TYPE=f32, which fixes
the output without this patch. llama.cpp's own graphs already request F32 accumulation where F16
overflows (kq, and ffn_down for GLM4, GLM4_MOE and JAIS2 in llama-graph.cpp); this does the
same for every weight matmul the projector builds through clip_graph::build_mm.

It is not specific to the shared ggml-sys build: llama-mtmd-cli built from pristine b11201 with
its bundled ggml fails identically. Upstream report: not yet filed; no fix on master as of
95887577 (2026-09-26), and no matching issue or pull request.

Validated against llama.cpp b11201 on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 Ti (CUDA 13.3, driver 595.84)
with llama-mtmd-cli, comparing the projector embeddings (MTMD_DEBUG_EMBEDDINGS) with the CPU
projector's:
- LightOnOCR-2-1B, a rendered paper page and complex_rotated_scans/rotated_scan_1.jpg: NaN and
  a degenerate answer before; after, relative L2 error 1.2 % and 2.6 %, no NaN, and the same
  greedy transcription as the CPU projector. Encoding one page takes 0.9 to 1.0 s instead of
  9.2 to 11.4 s on 24 CPU threads.
- Qwen3-VL-2B (F16 projector), which was already finite: relative L2 error 4.7 % before and
  4.0 % after, the same greedy answer, median encode time 2.1 s before and 2.6 s after (22 s
  on the CPU).
Restricting F32 to the feed-forward down projection alone also removes the NaN, but leaves
up to twice the deviation from the CPU projector (2.3 % and 3.7 %) for about 10 % less encode
time.

diff --git a/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp b/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp
index 62d146e..4e5e758 100644
--- a/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp
+++ b/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp
@@ -297,7 +297,14 @@ clip_graph::clip_graph(const clip_graph & parent) :
 }
 
 ggml_tensor * clip_graph::build_mm(ggml_tensor * w, ggml_tensor * x) const {
-    return ggml_mul_mat(ctx0, w, x);
+    ggml_tensor * cur = ggml_mul_mat(ctx0, w, x);
+    // Vision towers produce activations beyond the F16 range, most visibly in the feed-forward
+    // down projection of their last layers.
+    // The CUDA backend accumulates F16-weight matmuls in F16 by default, which overflows to inf
+    // and turns the image embeddings into NaN, so request F32 accumulation, which the CPU
+    // backend always uses.
+    ggml_prec_set_acc(cur, GGML_PREC_F32);
+    return cur;
 }
 
 void clip_graph::cb(ggml_tensor * cur, const char * name, int il) const {
